speechtext:
I am willing to do that;
I am willing to do anything. But what we want is two transcontinental lines. Extending the Intercolonial westward will not help it. Here is this great Grand Trunk Railway, with its ramifications all through the heart of the country, whose place can never be taken by any other railway, it has the command of a great body of internal trade which no other railway can get; and we want to make the Grand Trunk take the whole of that enormous trade which it commands and dump it at a Canadian port. Ultimately we shall have to have some control over the rates of these great transcontinental lines ; and perhaps the best way would be for the government to extend westward the road which they own, and in that way they would control these great lines in spite of fate. But that is not the question we are dealing with at present. If every man in this House takes a firm stand on this question, I am persuaded that Canada will occupy a better position than it does to-day. I do not care a fig about politics. I am a party man, as a rule, but on this question, when the interests of the country are at stake, I am not a party man.
I think the proper course is to agree upon some common point ; and if the Grand Trunk goes to Moncton, the men who are managing the line may be left to find the best port to use as a summer and winter port. If the men who are asking these enormous sums from Canada will come forward and say, we will make our port in the maritime provinces, the process of leaving Portland will have begun, and it will be gradually abandoned without any stringent or strong measures, which I would be averse to, because the Grand Trunk put its terminus at Portland in early times with the consent of parliament. We must give them consideration on that ground. I would be willing to go far to compensate them for any loss'they might incur in moving their terminals to Canada : but they must come to Canada, and that is the universal feeling in the maritime provinces. I am a Unionist, but if it comes to this, that any portion of this country is not going to be dealt fairly with by the country as a whole, I feel bound to insist that the rights of every part of the country should be respected. We in the maritime provinces have stood by the west, we have advocated the spending of money in order to develop the west, and we do so still, but it must be understood that the maritime provinces shall receive the support of the rest of Canada In their desire to make our all the year round Atlantic port on the Canadian coast. The whole thing simply amounts to this. If you are not going to carry out the compact of confederation, it is idle to have a confederation. If we are to make of confederation the success we hope to make it. we must see that justice is done to every portion of it, we must see that the union is maintained in its integrity, and that each and
